00:08:00,639 --> 00:08:07,319
If we wanted to, we could update these instructions manually and pass in new instructions.

128
00:08:07,319 --> 00:08:08,600
But that's kind of a manual update.

129
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:12,520
That's not really memory in terms of how we think about it in terms of agents.

130
00:08:12,520 --> 00:08:19,040
With agents, we think about an LLM updating these based on some user feedback.

131
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:22,240
So that's exactly what we're going to do.

132
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:27,840
So we're going to import from Langmem, this function called create multi prompt optimizer.

133
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:34,320
As the name suggests, this is going to create an optimizer that optimizes multiple prompts.

134
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:39,260
The reason we want to optimize multiple prompts is that in this assistant, there are actually

135
00:08:39,260 --> 00:08:43,700
four different prompt instructions that we have, and we might want to update any number

136
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:45,400
of them.

137
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:50,580
But we don't get feedback for each instruction, rather we get feedback for the whole

138
00:08:50,580 --> 00:08:53,320
